description:
In handle_metadata_set (src/metadata.rs:416-431), the destroy path pre-validates every element with:

    if let Some(bad) = destroy_arr.iter().find(|v| !v.is_string()) {
        return Err(...);
    }
    for id_val in destroy_arr {
        let id_str = match id_val.as_str() {
            Some(s) => s.to_owned(),
            None => continue, // unreachable: validated above
        };
        ...
    }

After the find() pre-check, the None branch is provably unreachable. The author signals awareness with the comment 'unreachable: validated above'. Idiomatic Rust would either:

(a) Use .expect() to make the invariant assertion explicit:

    let id_str = id_val.as_str()
        .expect('pre-validated as String by find() above')
        .to_owned();

(b) Restructure so the validation+iteration happens in one pass:

    let mut id_strs = Vec::with_capacity(destroy_arr.len());
    for id_val in &destroy_arr {
        match id_val.as_str() {
            Some(s) => id_strs.push(s.to_owned()),
            None => return Err(JmapError::invalid_arguments(format!(
                'destroy: every element must be a string Id; got {id_val}'
            ))),
        }
    }

(b) is the most idiomatic — one pass, no dead branch, no comment-as-assertion. (a) is acceptable too. As-written the dead 'None => continue' arm is a future-bug magnet: a future refactor that removes the find() pre-check leaves the silent skip in place.

What goes wrong: the silent 'continue' branch is the worst possible recovery if the invariant ever breaks — it would convert 'malformed request' into 'partial success with no error reported'. The comment is load-bearing; the invariant should be in the type system, not in the comment.
